      "What Text to Speech Really Does (And What This Tool Actually Is)",
      "Text to speech sounds like a small trick until the first time you actually need it. You have a long page of notes, an email draft you have rewritten four times, a chapter you keep re-reading without absorbing, or a message written in a language you are still learning. Your eyes are tired, but your ears are free. That is the simple idea behind this tool: you paste your text, choose a voice that already lives on your own phone or computer, set a comfortable speed and pitch, press Play, and the device reads the text back to you out loud, sentence by sentence, until it is done.",
      "This Text to Speech page is a free tool on a multilingual site with an interface in eleven languages. It does not use a distant AI server and it does not hide a robot in the cloud. It uses something your browser already has: the built-in speech synthesis feature, the same technology that powers screen readers and voice assistants at a basic level. That design choice matters, because it changes everything about privacy, cost, and honesty. Your text stays on your device. There is nothing to upload, nothing to wait for, and nothing stored on a server afterwards. It also means the tool has real limits, and this guide will explain those limits plainly instead of dressing them up. You will get listening, controls, and convenience.",
      "When Listening Beats Reading: Six Situations Where Your Ears Win",
      "The first situation is proofreading, and it surprises almost everyone who tries it. When you read your own writing silently, your brain helpfully skips over the mistakes because it already knows what you meant to say. When you hear the same text read aloud, the errors become impossible to ignore. A missing word suddenly sounds empty. A sentence that runs too long leaves the voice gasping for a pause that is not there. Repeated words, awkward phrasing, and punctuation problems all stand out in sound in a way they never do on the screen. Students use this before submitting assignments, job applicants use it on cover letters, and writers use it on drafts they have stared at for too long.",
