    "What this generator really does",
    "At its heart, this tool does one simple job: you give it a floor and a ceiling, and it hands back whole numbers drawn from somewhere inside that stretch, with no favourite and no memory of the last draw. Set the minimum to 1 and the maximum to 100, ask for six numbers, and you might get 4, 17, 38, 61, 83, 97 — or any other combination the range allows. Every value inside the range has the same chance of appearing, and that even-handedness is the entire point.",
    "That sounds trivial until you try to do it fairly by hand. Ask a person to 'pick a random number' and they avoid the edges, repeat pet numbers like 7, and unconsciously steer away from whatever came up a moment ago. A generator has none of those habits. It does not get embarrassed by picking 1 twice in a row, and it does not feel that 50 is 'too obvious' to choose from a 1-to-100 range. The result feels less human, which is exactly why it is more trustworthy.",
    "Two honest things belong right at the top, because random-number tools are often sold with a wink. First, this generator uses your browser's built-in cryptographic randomness — the same strong source your browser relies on for secure connections — but it is not 'true random' harvested from physical noise like atmospheric static or lava lamps, and this guide will never pretend otherwise. Second, a fair draw is not a lucky draw: nothing here can predict a lottery, improve your odds, or spot a pattern that does not exist. Fairness and fortune are different things, and only one of them is on offer.",
    "When a random draw helps — real use cases",
    "The classic scene is a classroom. A teacher needs three students to present first, and pointing at the front row every time quietly teaches the back row to stop preparing. Drawing roll numbers from a range makes the selection visibly neutral: nobody was picked on, nobody was skipped, and the argument is over before it starts. The same trick settles who presents, who answers the warm-up question, and whose project gets reviewed first when time runs short.",
    "At home and among friends, random draws end small negotiations that otherwise eat an evening. Two siblings both want the window seat, five friends cannot agree whose playlist runs the drive, a board-game group needs a starting player without the eternal 'you went first last time' debate. A coin flip or a quick 1-to-6 roll does not make the outcome better — it makes the outcome nobody's fault, which for small stakes is worth more than optimal. The tool's built-in coin and dice modes exist precisely for these moments.",
    "Organisers reach for a generator when they run small, informal giveaways: a book club picking next month's title from a shortlist, a streamer choosing a viewer from a chat list, a coach drawing practice-drill numbers. In each case the value is not the number itself but the visible fairness — everyone watched the same neutral process. The moment money, official prizes or legal obligations enter the picture, though, this casual tool steps aside; regulated draws need certified systems and audited procedures, and Section 5 says so plainly.",
    "Step by step: numbers, unique draws and sorting",
    "Start with the range. Minimum is the smallest number allowed, maximum the largest, and both ends are included — a range of 1 to 6 really can produce a 1 or a 6, just like a die. Type the values rather than trusting the defaults, because the range is where fairness is defined: if your class has 32 students, the range is 1 to 32, not 1 to 30 with two students silently excluded. If minimum is not smaller than maximum, the tool stops and tells you, instead of guessing what you meant.",
    "Next, choose how many numbers you want. One draw gives a single decider; several draws give you a set — team captains, question numbers, a shortlist. Then comes the most misunderstood switch on the page: allow repeats. With repeats on, each draw is independent, so the same number may appear twice, exactly as a real die can show 4 twice in a row. With repeats off, every number in your result is unique, which is what you want for picking distinct winners or students. The catch is arithmetic: with repeats off, you cannot draw more unique numbers than the range contains — asking for 12 unique numbers from 1 to 10 is impossible, and the tool will say so rather than quietly breaking its own rule.",
    "Sorting is pure presentation, and that is its virtue. Turning sort on shows your drawn numbers from low to high, which makes a list easier to read aloud, check against a register, or paste into a document. Turning it off preserves draw order, which matters when order itself is the result — first drawn presents first, second drawn goes second. Neither setting changes which numbers were drawn; it only changes how they line up for your eyes. Draw first, then decide which view serves the moment.",
    "When the numbers appear, check them against your purpose before announcing anything: right range, right count, repeats behaving as expected. The copy button copies the result exactly as shown, ready to paste into a chat, document or classroom screen, and every draw is added to the history panel below. That history exists so a draw can be verified after the fact — 'the third number was 27, scroll down' — without anyone having to trust a memory. If you drew with the wrong range, just draw again; a fresh draw costs nothing and accusing an old one helps nobody.",
    "Coin flip and dice modes inside the same tool",
    "Some decisions have only two faces, and the coin mode is built for them. One tap flips, the result lands as heads or tails, and running counts keep score across flips: how many heads, how many tails, how many flips in total. Watching those counts over twenty or fifty flips is the most convincing randomness lesson there is — the split drifts close to even without ever being forced, and long streaks of five heads happen more often than intuition expects. Streaks are not the coin misbehaving; they are what genuine independence looks like.",
    "The dice mode rolls between one and six dice at once and shows each die's face plus the total. One die settles a move or a turn; two dice reproduce the familiar board-game distribution where 7 is common and 2 is rare — a fact you can feel after a dozen rolls, no formula required. Three or more dice suit tabletop games and quick probability demonstrations. Because the faces come from the same cryptographic source as the number draws, a digital die here has no worn corners, no favourite landing spot, and no way to be thrown 'softly' on purpose.",
    "A practical tip for both modes: agree the meaning before you flip or roll. 'Heads, I choose the film; tails, you choose' only works if it is said out loud first, and 'highest total picks the restaurant' prevents the loser from inventing 'best of three' afterwards. The tool provides the fairness; the people provide the agreement. That tiny ritual — terms first, draw second — is what makes a random outcome feel binding instead of merely disappointing.",
    "How browser randomness works — honest limits",
    "Under the hood, this tool asks your browser for random bytes through a system called crypto.getRandomValues — the cryptographic random source built into every modern browser, fed by the operating system. It is the same family of randomness that protects secure logins and encrypted connections, and it is deliberately designed to be unpredictable: given past outputs, nobody can work out the next one. That is a far stronger foundation than the quick Math.random function found in casual scripts, which is built for speed and simulations, not unpredictability — which is why this tool never uses it.",
    "Turning random bytes into a number between your minimum and maximum needs care, and one subtle trap is worth naming because honest tools avoid it: if you simply divide the random range carelessly, some values in your range end up slightly more likely than others — a flaw called modulo bias. This generator uses rejection sampling instead: it draws values, discards the few that would fall in the uneven leftover zone, and draws again. The discards cost a fraction of a millisecond and buy exact evenness — every number in your range genuinely equally likely.",
    "Now the limits, stated without fine print. This is browser cryptographic randomness, not 'true random' from a physical noise source, and it carries no certificate. It is therefore not approved for regulated gambling, official lotteries, legal prize draws, court or compliance selections, or anything where an auditor must certify the random source. Those jobs need certified hardware, published procedures and independent testing, and no honest web page can offer them from a browser tab. If your draw has legal or financial consequences, use the official channel built for it.",
    "The other limit is about expectation. A fair generator cannot predict anything: it cannot find 'hot' numbers, avoid 'due' ones, or improve lottery chances by a single fraction. Past draws leave no fingerprint on future ones — after ten flips land heads eight times, the eleventh flip is still an even chance, and any tool claiming otherwise is selling a story, not statistics. What this tool promises is narrower and real: an unbiased draw, made locally, that nobody at the table can steer.",
    "Privacy: nothing leaves your device",
    "Every calculation described in this guide happens inside your browser tab. Your minimum and maximum, the numbers drawn, your coin streaks and dice totals are never sent to a server, because the tool has no server doing the work — the page arrives once, and after that your device does everything itself. There is no account collecting your draws, no analytics payload carrying your results away, and nothing to leak from a database that does not exist.",
    "The history panel follows the same rule. It is session memory only: it lives in the tab so you can scroll back and verify a draw, it is visible to anyone looking at your screen — worth remembering on a shared classroom display — and it disappears completely when the tab closes. The clear-history button wipes it sooner if you want a clean slate mid-session. Nothing in the history is saved to an account, synced to another device, or kept for a return visit. Tomorrow's session starts blank, exactly as it should.",
    "Questions and answers + closing",
    "Is this the same as asking someone to pick a number? No — people favour round numbers, birthdays and the middle of the range, while the generator treats every value alike, including the unfashionable edges. Can the same number really appear twice? With repeats on, yes, and it should: blocking a repeat would make the next draw less random, not more. Why did the tool refuse my draw? Almost always because the range was upside down, or because you asked for more unique numbers than the range holds with repeats off — both messages explain themselves, and widening the range or reducing the count fixes it.",
    "Can I use this for my shop's prize draw or a raffle? For a small, informal, no-stakes giveaway among friends or a club, people do — but for anything official, paid-entry, regulated or legally contestable, no: use a certified draw service with an audit trail, because this browser tool is not certified for gambling, lotteries or legal prize draws. Will using it a lot reveal a pattern? No pattern exists to reveal; each draw is independent, and the running coin counts will hover near even without ever being forced there. Does anything get uploaded? Nothing — ranges, results and history stay in the tab and vanish when it closes.",
    "Is the coin really fair over time? Fair means each flip is unpredictable and even-chanced, not that ten flips must split exactly five-five; small samples wobble, large samples settle, and the counters let you watch that happen yourself. What if I need the same 'random' result again for a test? This tool is the wrong instrument — reproducibility needs a seeded generator in code, where the same seed repeats the same sequence; here, unrepeatability is the point. Can I draw letters or names? Not in this tool — shuffle your list externally, then draw positions or numbers to pick entries by index.",
    "A random number is a small, polite way of saying: no one chose this. Used well, it ends arguments in classrooms, starts games without ceremony, samples honestly and picks fairly among friends. This generator keeps that promise with strong browser cryptography, exact even odds with no hidden bias, coin and dice at your fingertips, and the discipline to forget everything when you close the tab. Set your range, agree the terms out loud, and press Generate — the rest is chance, fairly dealt."
