    "Three formats, three different promises",
    "Every image conversion problem starts the same way: a website rejects your upload, a client asks for a PNG you do not have, or your page loads slowly under heavy photos. The fix is rarely a different picture — it is a different container for the same picture. JPG, PNG and WebP are three containers with three different promises. JPG promises a small, universally readable photo file and gives up transparency and a little quality to keep that promise. PNG promises exactness: every pixel preserved, transparency included, at the price of a much heavier file. WebP is the modern compromise: photo-style compression with transparency support, built for the web. Converting does not improve or redraw your image. It re-encodes the same pixels under a different set of rules, at exactly the same width and height. Once that is clear, choosing stops being guesswork.",
    "Convert, resize, compress — name the job before you click",
    "People waste the most time by asking for the wrong operation. Converting changes the format and nothing else: a 3000 by 2000 JPG becomes a 3000 by 2000 PNG, pixel for pixel. Resizing changes the geometry: the same photo becomes 1200 by 800. Compressing keeps both format family and dimensions and only squeezes the encoding harder so the file weighs less. This page converts. If your photo is the wrong dimensions for the slot, use the Image Resizer on this site first, then convert the result if you still need a different format. If the dimensions and format are already right and only the file is too heavy for an upload limit, the Image Compressor is the shorter path. Naming the job takes five seconds and saves the familiar loop of converting, uploading, being rejected, and starting again.",
    "When JPG to PNG actually helps — and when it only makes a bigger file",
    "JPG to PNG is the most requested conversion and the most misunderstood. It helps in exactly two situations. First, when editing software or a print or design workflow demands PNG as its input, and the file must be delivered in that container whatever it costs. Second, as a working step: if you plan to edit a JPG repeatedly, saving an intermediate PNG stops each edit from re-compressing the photo and compounding the damage, because PNG never throws pixels away. What JPG to PNG cannot do is restore quality. JPG discarded fine detail the moment the photo was first saved, and no converter can invent it back. The PNG will be several times larger and look identical. Sending it to someone as proof of higher quality is a bigger envelope with the same letter inside. Convert for compatibility or as an editing waypoint, never as an upgrade.",
    "PNG to JPG: the transparency decision you cannot undo later",
    "PNG to JPG is where careful people pause, because this direction deletes something real. PNG can store fully and partly transparent pixels — the invisible background around a logo, the soft edge of a cut-out product. JPG has no transparency channel at all. The converter must therefore paint something behind your image before saving, and this tool lets you choose: white by default, black, or any custom colour. White suits documents and most marketplaces; black suits dark designs; a brand colour suits overlays that will sit on a known background. The warning in the tool matters: once flattened onto white, the transparency is gone from that copy forever. Keep the PNG master untouched, convert a copy for delivery, and if there is any chance the image will be placed on a coloured background later, deliver PNG or WebP instead and let the receiver flatten it themselves.",
    "WebP for websites, JPG for everywhere else",
    "JPG to WebP and PNG to WebP exist for one reason: page weight. At the same visual quality, WebP photos are commonly noticeably smaller than their JPG equivalents, and WebP also supports transparency, so one format can replace both JPG photos and many PNG graphics on a modern website. Every current major browser displays WebP. The honest limits are social and practical, not technical: some older apps, some office software, and the occasional upload form still refuse WebP, and a client opening your file in an old program will blame you, not their software. The working rule used by most site owners is simple. Publish WebP on your own pages where speed matters. Send JPG when the file must open absolutely anywhere — email, forms, marketplaces, a stranger's phone. Convert WebP back to JPG or PNG (WebP to JPG, WebP to PNG) when a downloaded web image needs to enter a workflow that does not speak WebP yet.",
    "What the quality slider really trades",
    "For JPG and WebP output you get a quality slider, and it deserves respect because it is the only destructive choice on the page. At 100 the encoder keeps almost everything and the file stays heavy. Around 80 to 85 most photos become much lighter while the difference remains invisible at normal viewing size — this is the everyday sweet spot. Push toward 60 and below and the savings continue but the artefacts arrive: skies band into steps, text edges fray, faces waxy. PNG ignores the slider completely, because lossless means lossless; that is why a PNG photo can be heavier than the JPG it came from. Quality is per-conversion and not recoverable: re-encoding a quality-60 JPG at quality 100 produces a faithful copy of a damaged picture, bigger file included. Choose once, choose deliberately, and keep the original for the day you need a different trade-off.",
    "Batch conversion without the usual batch regrets",
    "The queue on this page exists because real jobs arrive in folders, not singles: thirty product photos for a shop, a semester of screenshots, a photographer's export. Add them all, check the detected format shown beside each name — the tool reads the actual file, not just its extension, so a PNG renamed to .jpg is caught — pick one output format and quality for the batch, and convert everything in one pass. Before downloading, read the before and after sizes per file and in total. That panel is your audit: a PNG that ballooned after JPG-to-PNG conversion, or a photo that barely shrank, is visible before it costs you an upload. Download files one by one where you need them, or use download-all, which saves each converted file separately in turn. Browsers may ask permission when several downloads follow each other; allow it once and the queue completes.",
    "The HEIC wall, GIF surprises and other honest friction",
    "Sooner or later an iPhone owner drops in a photo and nothing happens. That is the HEIC wall: Apple phones save HEIC or HEIF by default, and standard browser image decoding cannot read that format on most systems. This tool marks the file unreadable and tells you plainly to export or share the photo as JPG from the phone first — any browser converter faces the same wall unless it ships an extra decoding library, and we will not pretend otherwise. Other friction is quieter. An animated GIF or WebP converts as its first frame, a still image, because Canvas encodes stills. An SVG converts at the size the browser rasterises it. AVIF may open as input in some browsers and fail in others. Metadata such as camera EXIF is not carried into the Canvas output, which quietly suits privacy and quietly ruins workflows that needed it. None of this is hidden in fine print here; the support note above the tool states it before you start.",
    "A five-minute checklist for conversions that never bounce",
    "One: name the job — format, dimensions, or weight — and open the matching tool. Two: confirm the destination's real requirement from its own uploader or help page, not from memory. Three: if the destination is JPG and your source has transparency, choose the background colour deliberately and keep the transparent master. Four: set quality near 80 to 85 for photos unless a hard file-size limit forces you lower, and never re-convert a low-quality copy hoping to restore it. Five: convert a copy, compare before and after sizes and view the result at full size, especially edges and text. Six: deliver, then archive the original untouched. Follow those six steps and the humble act of changing a file's container stops producing rejected uploads, surprise black backgrounds and mysteriously ballooning folders — which is all a converter was ever supposed to do."
