    "What OCR actually does to your photo",
    "Optical Character Recognition is often described as if the computer simply reads. It does not. A printed page arrives as a grid of light and dark pixels, and before a single letter can be guessed, the engine must decide which pixels belong together at all. It first normalises the image for contrast and size, then hunts for lines of text, then for the gaps that split a line into words, then for the smaller gaps that split a word into characters. Only after that segmentation does the real reading start: each isolated shape is compared against what the engine has learned a letter can look like in the chosen language, and the most plausible character wins. Words are then checked against a vocabulary, because a shape that could be a lowercase L or the digit one is far more likely to be a letter inside a word. Every stage can fail independently: harsh shadows poison the contrast step, decorative fonts defeat the shape step, and a language pack that does not match the script leaves the vocabulary step guessing. That is why OCR is best understood as a chain of modest decisions rather than a single act of reading — and why preparing the image and picking the right language pack matter as much as the engine itself.",
    "Printed text, handwriting and the honesty rule",
    "The most important honest sentence about OCR is this: printed text and handwriting are not the same job. A printed letter is drawn from a small family of shapes, repeated almost identically thousands of times, and decades of tuning have made engines genuinely good at it when the image is clean, straight and well lit. Handwriting breaks every assumption that makes printing easy: no two of your letter a are shaped alike, strokes join unpredictably, words lean, and the vocabulary check cannot rescue shapes it never isolated correctly. This page therefore makes you a promise in the negative: it will never quote a fixed accuracy percentage, because any such number without a stated test image, font, language and lighting is marketing, not measurement. Commercial pages that advertise near-perfect scores for handwriting are asking you to trust a number nobody can reproduce. Here, the output box stays editable precisely so the human remains the final reader: the engine drafts, you verify. If your source is handwritten class notes, treat the result as a rough first pass you will correct heavily — or type the important parts yourself, which is sometimes honestly faster.",
    "Before you upload: five minutes of preparation that pay off",
    "Almost every bad OCR result is a bad photograph wearing a disguise. Four fixes recover most failures. First: light. Even, bright light without glare lets the contrast step see letters instead of shadows; a desk lamp pointed away from a glossy page beats a camera flash pointed at it. Second: geometry. Photograph the page square-on, fill the frame with text, and keep lines horizontal, because engines assume text runs in straight rows and skewed lines scramble word order. Third: resolution. Small print needs pixels; a tight crop of the paragraph you actually need will read better than a wide shot of a whole desk, and it also keeps unrelated text out of the result. Fourth: cleanliness. Crop away borders, fingers, logos and neighbouring columns, then choose the language pack that matches the script in the picture — not the language you wish it were. A Spanish page read with the English pack will systematically confuse accents and common words; a Japanese page read with a Latin pack will return junk with total confidence. These five minutes are unglamorous, and they are the entire difference between a result you lightly proofread and one you rewrite.",
    "Languages, scripts and the Roman Urdu trap",
    "This tool offers only language packs that genuinely exist for the Tesseract engine: English, Spanish, German, French, Turkish, Portuguese, Italian, Dutch, Norwegian, Japanese and Urdu. That list hides a real divide. Latin-script languages share most letter shapes, so a clean photo in any of them reads respectably. Japanese is materially harder: thousands of possible characters, no spaces between words, and vertical text in some layouts, so expect to proofread carefully even from a sharp image. Urdu in its own script is also harder than clean Latin, because joined, context-shaped letters resist the clean splitting that Latin printing allows. The subtlest trap is Roman Urdu — Urdu written in Latin letters, as in texts and chats. It looks like English to the engine's shape matcher but follows Urdu grammar and vocabulary, so the English pack will confidently return English-looking nonsense in places, while the Urdu pack expects a script that is not there. The honest answer is that no pack is trained for Roman Urdu; pick English, expect errors in names and local words, and proofread as if the engine were a fast but careless typist. Naming that limit plainly is the point of this page.",
    "Privacy: what stays, what downloads, and what never happens",
    "The recognition on this page runs inside your browser tab with Tesseract.js, compiled to WebAssembly. Your image is decoded and read on your own device, and nothing about it — not the file, not the extracted text — is sent to a server run by this site. There is one honest exception to the no-network story, and this page states it instead of hiding it: the OCR engine and the language data you choose are program files, and they have to come from somewhere the first time. On first use they download from a content delivery network and are cached by your browser afterwards, so the first run needs an internet connection and will fail offline; later runs of the same language can work from cache. Downloading data files is not uploading your image, and you can verify that in your browser's network tab: the requests are for engine and language files, never for your photo. If even that first download is unacceptable for a sensitive document, the correct alternative is desktop OCR software installed locally — a slower path, but one where nothing is fetched at all.",
    "A workflow for results you can actually trust",
    "Treat OCR as a draft machine and build a small routine around it. Prepare the photo with the light, geometry, resolution and crop checks. Pick the language pack that matches the script, run the read, and watch the progress states: engine start, language download, then recognition — a stall in the first two usually means the first-use download is struggling, not that your image is cursed. When text arrives, proofread against the original in three passes. Pass one: numbers, dates, totals and names, because a single confused digit on a receipt or an exam roll number is the error that actually hurts and the one spell-checkers never flag. Pass two: punctuation and line breaks, which OCR routinely flattens; tables in particular return as flowing text, so rebuild columns by hand if the layout mattered. Pass three: anything you intend to quote, submit or publish, read character by character. Then copy or download the corrected text and keep the original image filed beside it, because the photo remains the only ground truth if a figure is ever questioned later.",
    "When not to use this tool at all",
    "Honest tools say no sometimes. Do not use browser OCR for documents whose errors carry legal or financial weight — contracts, medical records, bank statements — without verifying every field against the original through a second channel; the draft may look clean while one digit quietly changed. Do not expect it to recover text from screenshots of screenshots, tiny chat thumbnails, or photos taken at an angle through glass: information destroyed before the engine starts cannot be re-invented by it. Handwritten archives, decorative poster fonts, and heavily stylised logos will disappoint, and a PDF is not an image at all — export the single page you need as JPG or PNG first, or use a proper PDF tool. Finally, remember the tool processes one image at a time by design: a sixty-page scan deserves dedicated desktop software with batch handling, not sixty patient clicks here. Choosing the right tool for the job is not a failure of this one; it is exactly the judgement this guide exists to teach."
